WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.210 --> 00:00:01.320
MARK MIRCHANDANI: One of
the greatest advantages

00:00:01.320 --> 00:00:03.030
of the cloud is
how easy it can be

00:00:03.030 --> 00:00:05.380
to scale your usage up or down.

00:00:05.380 --> 00:00:08.250
However, if you're working with
a predictable compute workload,

00:00:08.250 --> 00:00:11.100
you can save some money with
committed use discounts.

00:00:11.100 --> 00:00:12.760
On this episode of
"Beyond Your Bill,"

00:00:12.760 --> 00:00:14.880
we'll talk about what
committed use discounts are

00:00:14.880 --> 00:00:17.130
and how you can maximize
your usage of them.

00:00:17.130 --> 00:00:19.580
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:24.980 --> 00:00:28.060
Committed Use Discounts,
or CUDs for short,

00:00:28.060 --> 00:00:29.660
are basically
contracts saying you'll

00:00:29.660 --> 00:00:31.970
commit to a certain amount
of Compute Engine resource

00:00:31.970 --> 00:00:34.370
for a one or three-year period.

00:00:34.370 --> 00:00:36.170
In exchange for your
commitment, you'll

00:00:36.170 --> 00:00:39.320
get a significant price
discount, even as high as 70%,

00:00:39.320 --> 00:00:40.760
in some cases.

00:00:40.760 --> 00:00:43.490
You can make these commitments
on typical compute resources

00:00:43.490 --> 00:00:48.872
such as vCPUs and memory, but
also on GPUs and local SSDs.

00:00:48.872 --> 00:00:51.080
It's worth noting that
committed use discounts aren't

00:00:51.080 --> 00:00:53.690
the same thing as sustained
use discounts, which

00:00:53.690 --> 00:00:55.670
are separate discounts
that can't be combined

00:00:55.670 --> 00:00:57.380
with committed use discounts.

00:00:57.380 --> 00:00:59.665
There's a link below with
more information on those.

00:00:59.665 --> 00:01:01.790
Let's look a bit more on
how to set up commitments.

00:01:01.790 --> 00:01:04.250
And then explore the committed
use discount analysis

00:01:04.250 --> 00:01:07.430
report, which allows you to
visualize your commitments.

00:01:07.430 --> 00:01:10.080
This report is meant to help
you answer questions like,

00:01:10.080 --> 00:01:12.200
how much of my eligible
Compute Engine usage

00:01:12.200 --> 00:01:13.940
is covered by my commitments?

00:01:13.940 --> 00:01:16.280
How much are my commitments
actually saving me?

00:01:16.280 --> 00:01:19.143
And am I fully utilizing
my existing commitments?

00:01:19.143 --> 00:01:21.560
To start, you can purchase
commitments in the Google Cloud

00:01:21.560 --> 00:01:24.080
Console by using the
top left navigation

00:01:24.080 --> 00:01:27.320
and clicking on Committed Use
Discounts under Compute Engine.

00:01:27.320 --> 00:01:29.180
From here, click on
Purchase Commitment

00:01:29.180 --> 00:01:31.680
to enter the specifics,
such as the time frame

00:01:31.680 --> 00:01:33.800
and which resource
you want to purchase.

00:01:33.800 --> 00:01:37.370
Each commitment is purchased for
a specific project and region.

00:01:37.370 --> 00:01:39.080
Commitments for
vCPUs and memories

00:01:39.080 --> 00:01:41.180
are applied
simultaneously, but you

00:01:41.180 --> 00:01:44.780
can have separate commitments
for GPUs and local SSDs.

00:01:44.780 --> 00:01:46.330
You'll also get
an estimated cost

00:01:46.330 --> 00:01:48.410
to let you see how much
you'll pay each month

00:01:48.410 --> 00:01:50.870
and how much the
commitment would save you.

00:01:50.870 --> 00:01:53.270
If you run into issues
purchasing a commitment,

00:01:53.270 --> 00:01:55.710
you may need to make sure
that you have a quota for it.

00:01:55.710 --> 00:01:58.155
There's also a link below with
more information for that.

00:01:58.155 --> 00:01:59.780
Once you've made the
commitment, you'll

00:01:59.780 --> 00:02:01.863
start to pay for that
commitment over its lifetime

00:02:01.863 --> 00:02:03.860
with each billing
cycle, even if you don't

00:02:03.860 --> 00:02:05.720
have any resources active.

00:02:05.720 --> 00:02:08.270
In this example, a certain
amount of vCPUs and RAM

00:02:08.270 --> 00:02:10.492
have been used by a
Compute Engine instance.

00:02:10.492 --> 00:02:12.200
The amount that's
covered by a commitment

00:02:12.200 --> 00:02:14.360
will be credited back and
the lower commitment rate

00:02:14.360 --> 00:02:16.580
will be applied for that usage.

00:02:16.580 --> 00:02:19.328
However, if you've got lots
of resources and commitments,

00:02:19.328 --> 00:02:21.620
it can be confusing to see
exactly what you're spending

00:02:21.620 --> 00:02:23.550
and which commitments
you have active,

00:02:23.550 --> 00:02:26.120
especially since commitments
don't auto renew.

00:02:26.120 --> 00:02:28.190
That's where the committed
use discount analysis

00:02:28.190 --> 00:02:29.423
report comes in.

00:02:29.423 --> 00:02:31.340
It helps you visualize
all of your commitments

00:02:31.340 --> 00:02:33.560
alongside your
compute resources.

00:02:33.560 --> 00:02:35.240
Using the Google
Cloud Console, choose

00:02:35.240 --> 00:02:37.480
a billing account and click
on the Commitments option

00:02:37.480 --> 00:02:38.492
to get to the report.

00:02:38.492 --> 00:02:39.950
You'll need to be
a billing account

00:02:39.950 --> 00:02:42.020
administrator or a
billing account viewer

00:02:42.020 --> 00:02:43.400
to see this report.

00:02:43.400 --> 00:02:44.930
Unlike billing
reports, you won't

00:02:44.930 --> 00:02:46.550
be able to get
access to this report

00:02:46.550 --> 00:02:49.100
just by being a project
owner, though you will

00:02:49.100 --> 00:02:50.997
be able to filter by project.

00:02:50.997 --> 00:02:52.580
Let's step through
the different areas

00:02:52.580 --> 00:02:54.740
to see how the report works.

00:02:54.740 --> 00:02:56.580
The resources are
split up by type.

00:02:56.580 --> 00:03:00.720
So you can see vCPU, RAM,
SSD, and each type of GPU

00:03:00.720 --> 00:03:04.190
separately with your usage
and the relevant commitments.

00:03:04.190 --> 00:03:06.680
You can toggle between them
by clicking the tabs here,

00:03:06.680 --> 00:03:08.930
but we'll start with vCPUs.

00:03:08.930 --> 00:03:10.910
Above the graph, you
can see a quick summary

00:03:10.910 --> 00:03:12.230
of your commitments.

00:03:12.230 --> 00:03:15.050
The left side lets you see if
you're looking at all regions

00:03:15.050 --> 00:03:17.720
or if you're only looking
at a specific region, which

00:03:17.720 --> 00:03:19.970
I'll show you how to
do in just a second.

00:03:19.970 --> 00:03:21.500
The middle shows
the current number

00:03:21.500 --> 00:03:23.360
of active commitments,
which may take

00:03:23.360 --> 00:03:26.500
a day or two to update when a
new commitment is purchased.

00:03:26.500 --> 00:03:29.000
The right side shows you the
utilization of your commitments

00:03:29.000 --> 00:03:31.580
by comparing the amount of
eligible usage being covered

00:03:31.580 --> 00:03:33.830
by your commitments to the
total number of commitments

00:03:33.830 --> 00:03:35.032
that you have.

00:03:35.032 --> 00:03:36.740
You can also hover
over the question mark

00:03:36.740 --> 00:03:39.060
for the specific
resource counts.

00:03:39.060 --> 00:03:40.820
The stacked bar chart
itself shows you

00:03:40.820 --> 00:03:43.160
the resource uses for
each day, the amount that

00:03:43.160 --> 00:03:45.440
was covered by a
commitment, and a line that

00:03:45.440 --> 00:03:47.930
shows you the current
quantity of commitments.

00:03:47.930 --> 00:03:50.330
You can hover over each
column to see the exact usage

00:03:50.330 --> 00:03:51.750
numbers for every day.

00:03:51.750 --> 00:03:54.330
There's also a summary
table below the chart.

00:03:54.330 --> 00:03:56.900
Let's take a look at a
specific subset of days.

00:03:56.900 --> 00:03:59.840
Here you can see the usage
narrowed down to a few days.

00:03:59.840 --> 00:04:01.400
On the first day,
all commitments

00:04:01.400 --> 00:04:02.460
were fully utilized.

00:04:02.460 --> 00:04:04.520
And there were additional
eligible resource usage

00:04:04.520 --> 00:04:06.650
on top of your
purchase commitments.

00:04:06.650 --> 00:04:09.290
If you often see resource
usage above your additional

00:04:09.290 --> 00:04:11.510
commitments, it may
make sense to purchase

00:04:11.510 --> 00:04:14.780
additional commitments to cover
that usage and save some money.

00:04:14.780 --> 00:04:17.240
However, you can also see
that on the next few days,

00:04:17.240 --> 00:04:20.420
the commitments haven't changed,
but the amount used has.

00:04:20.420 --> 00:04:22.160
Since this is the
cloud, it makes sense

00:04:22.160 --> 00:04:24.120
that resource usage
can fluctuate.

00:04:24.120 --> 00:04:26.420
But how can we find out
why these resources weren't

00:04:26.420 --> 00:04:27.530
covered?

00:04:27.530 --> 00:04:29.610
Let's drill down
using the filters.

00:04:29.610 --> 00:04:31.400
You can also change
the time ranges here,

00:04:31.400 --> 00:04:33.080
if you want to see more dates.

00:04:33.080 --> 00:04:36.020
We'll change the view to Region
and see the specifics split up

00:04:36.020 --> 00:04:37.520
by each region.

00:04:37.520 --> 00:04:39.590
This is the US
Central One region,

00:04:39.590 --> 00:04:42.428
but the graph is still showing
the same usage pattern.

00:04:42.428 --> 00:04:43.970
Remember that
committed use discounts

00:04:43.970 --> 00:04:46.530
are specific to a
project and a region.

00:04:46.530 --> 00:04:50.150
So let's use the filters to
select a specific project.

00:04:50.150 --> 00:04:52.830
With this filter, we can
see that less vCPUs were

00:04:52.830 --> 00:04:55.070
used for a few days
in this project, which

00:04:55.070 --> 00:04:57.620
led to the commitment
being underutilized.

00:04:57.620 --> 00:05:00.350
Understanding more details
about commitment utilization

00:05:00.350 --> 00:05:02.360
can give you a much
better idea of how to plan

00:05:02.360 --> 00:05:04.250
for future usage of resources.

00:05:04.250 --> 00:05:06.080
Or even where you
may want to purchase

00:05:06.080 --> 00:05:07.870
additional commitments.

00:05:07.870 --> 00:05:09.620
As you've seen, committed
to use discounts

00:05:09.620 --> 00:05:12.240
can save you money on your
Compute Engine resources,

00:05:12.240 --> 00:05:14.807
especially if you have a
lot of consistent usage.

00:05:14.807 --> 00:05:17.390
It's also important to use the
committed use discount analysis

00:05:17.390 --> 00:05:20.480
report to see how your existing
commitments are sizing up

00:05:20.480 --> 00:05:21.890
and if it makes
sense to purchase

00:05:21.890 --> 00:05:23.243
additional commitments.

00:05:23.243 --> 00:05:24.910
Check out the links
for more information

00:05:24.910 --> 00:05:27.490
and try out the report for
yourself in the GCP console.

00:05:27.490 --> 00:05:30.240
[MUSIC PLAYING]

